<releaseinfo>dolphinCommander (based on Dolphin from KDE Gear 26.11)</releaseinfo>

<abstract>
<para>
dolphinCommander is a file manager with two sides next to each other. Each side has its own tabs,
its own path bar and its own Miller columns. It is a fork of Dolphin.
</para>
</abstract>

<keywordset>
<keyword>dolphinCommander</keyword>
<keyword>Dolphin</keyword>
<keyword>file manager</keyword>
<keyword>two panes</keyword>
<keyword>Miller columns</keyword>
</keywordset>

</bookinfo>

<chapter id="introduction">
<title>Introduction</title>

<para>
dolphinCommander is a fork of Dolphin, the file manager of KDE, and works more like
OneCommander. The window always has two sides. Each side shows a folder and has everything
that is needed to move around: tabs, a path bar, and a strip of columns with the parent folders.
Moving or copying files from one side to the other is a single key press.
</para>

<para>
Most of what Dolphin can do is still here: the view modes, sorting and grouping, previews,
the context menu, the Places and Terminal panels, and the settings for them. This handbook describes
what is different and how to use it.
</para>

<para>
dolphinCommander is an independent project and is not affiliated with or endorsed by KDE. It can be
installed next to Dolphin and does not share its settings.
</para>
</chapter>

<chapter id="window">
<title>The window</title>

<sect1 id="panes">
<title>The two sides</title>

<para>
The window is split into a left and a right side by a divider. Both sides work the same way. One of
them is the <emphasis>active</emphasis> side, which is the one you clicked in last. The ribbon, the
keyboard and the status bar act on the active side.
</para>

<para>
In the middle of the divider are two arrows. The left arrow moves the divider to the left and so hides the left
side, the right arrow hides the right side. The divider then stays at the edge of the window, and its arrow
brings the hidden side back. You can also drag the divider all the way to an edge. Which side is hidden is
remembered with the rest of the session. If you hide the side that is active, the other side becomes active.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="tabs">
<title>Tabs</title>

<para>
Each side has its own tab bar, which is always shown, so that a new tab is one click away on the
<guibutton>+</guibutton> button. Each tab shows its own folder.
</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>Open a new tab on the active side with <guibutton>+</guibutton> or <keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>T</keycap></keycombo>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Close the current tab with <keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>W</keycap></keycombo>. The last tab of a side is not closed. If both sides are down to one tab each, closing it closes the window.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Reopen the last closed tab with <keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Shift;<keycap>T</keycap></keycombo>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Switch to the next or previous tab of the active side with <keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>Tab</keycap></keycombo> and <keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Shift;<keycap>Tab</keycap></keycombo>.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="location-bar">
<title>The path bar</title>

<para>
Above each side is a path bar that shows the full path of the folder as a row of buttons. Click one of
the buttons to go to that folder. Click on the empty space behind them, or press
<keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>L</keycap></keycombo>, to type a path instead. Press <keycap>Enter</keycap>
to go there.
</para>

<para>
Next to the path bar are the buttons <guibutton>Back</guibutton>, <guibutton>Forward</guibutton>,
<guibutton>Up</guibutton>, <guibutton>Refresh</guibutton> and <guibutton>Search</guibutton>, in the row of the ribbon tabs.
<keycap>Backspace</keycap> goes back. Double-clicking on empty space in a folder goes up one folder. You can
change that in the settings.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="miller-columns">
<title>Miller columns</title>

<para>
Between the path bar and the file list is a strip with one column for each of the parent folders of the
current folder. Every column lists the contents of its folder and highlights the folder that leads to the
next column. Click a folder in a column to go there. Click a file to open it.
</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>Drag the divider between two columns to make a column wider or narrower. The widths are kept for each level of the path as long as the window is open.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>The columns share the width of the side. If there is not enough room for all of them, the columns that are farthest back are hidden, so that you never have to scroll the strip sideways.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Turn the strip off in the settings, on the <guilabel>Interface</guilabel> page under <guilabel>Status &amp; Location bars</guilabel>, with the option <guilabel>Show the parent folders as columns above the file list</guilabel>.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="ribbon">
<title>The ribbon</title>

<para>
The ribbon at the top of the window replaces the menus and the toolbar. It has one tab for each of
<guimenu>File</guimenu>, <guimenu>Edit</guimenu>, <guimenu>View</guimenu>, <guimenu>Tools</guimenu> and
<guimenu>Settings</guimenu>, and the buttons of a tab are what the menu of that name offers. A button with
a small arrow opens a menu. The ribbon is as tall as one row of buttons for every tab.
</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<chapter id="files">
<title>Working with files</title>

<sect1 id="copy-move">
<title>Copying and moving between the sides</title>

<para>
Select files on one side and press <keycap>F5</keycap> to copy them to the folder that the other side shows,
or <keycap>F6</keycap> to move them there. If nothing is selected, the view changes to selection mode, where you
can pick the files. <guimenuitem>Open in Other Pane</guimenuitem> in the context menu of a folder shows that
folder on the other side. <keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>F3</keycap></keycombo> moves the keyboard focus to the other side.
</para>

<para>
Dragging files from one side to the other works as well. If the other side is hidden, it is shown first when you
use <guimenuitem>Open in Other Pane</guimenuitem> or move the focus there.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="filtering">
<title>Filtering by typing</title>

<para>
Start typing while the file list has the focus, and the filter bar opens above the list with what you typed. The
list is reduced to the items that match. Keep typing to narrow it down.
</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><keycap>Enter</keycap> moves the focus to the list and keeps the filter.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><keycap>Esc</keycap> closes the filter bar and removes the filter. One press is enough, in the filter bar as well as in the list.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>The filter bar can match plain text, a glob pattern or a regular expression, and it can be case sensitive. The lock button keeps the filter when you change the folder.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>I</keycap></keycombo> or <keycap>/</keycap> opens the filter bar without typing anything into it.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<para>
This replaces the search-as-you-type of Dolphin, which jumped to the first item that matched. The small
lists of the Miller columns still jump.
</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<chapter id="settings">
<title>Settings</title>

<para>
Open the settings with the <guibutton>Settings</guibutton> button on the <guimenu>Settings</guimenu> tab
of the ribbon. The pages are:
</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Interface</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>What is shown at start-up, whether new folders open in tabs, the tab style and where new tabs open, the previews, the confirmations, and the status bar and location bar. This is also where the Miller columns are switched on and off.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>View</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>How files are shown in each view mode and which action a double-click on empty space triggers. By default it goes up one folder.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Context Menu</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>Which entries the context menu shows, and the shortcuts for them.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Shortcuts</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The keyboard shortcuts of all actions, with a search field. Single letters are not allowed as shortcuts, as typing in a folder is used for filtering.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Language</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>The language of the application. The button opens the dialog in which it is chosen, and the change is used the next time the application is started.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Trash</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>How the trash is cleaned up.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>
The settings are kept in <filename>~/.config/dolphincommanderrc</filename>.
</para>
</chapter>

<chapter id="shortcuts">
<title>Keyboard shortcuts</title>

<informaltable>
<tgroup cols="2">
<thead>
<row><entry>Shortcut</entry><entry>Action</entry></row>
</thead>
<tbody>
<row><entry><keycap>F5</keycap></entry><entry>Copy the selection to the other side</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycap>F6</keycap></entry><entry>Move the selection to the other side</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>F3</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Move the focus to the other side</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>T</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>New tab on the active side</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>W</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Close the tab</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Shift;<keycap>T</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Reopen the last closed tab</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>Tab</keycap></keycombo>, <keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;&Shift;<keycap>Tab</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Next tab, previous tab</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>L</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Type a path in the path bar</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>R</keycap></keycombo></entry><entry>Refresh</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycap>Backspace</keycap></entry><entry>Back</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>I</keycap></keycombo>, <keycap>/</keycap></entry><entry>Open the filter bar</entry></row>
<row><entry><keycap>Esc</keycap></entry><entry>Close the filter bar</entry></row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</informaltable>

<para>
You can change the shortcuts on the <guilabel>Shortcuts</guilabel> page of the settings.
</para>
</chapter>

<chapter id="differences">
<title>Differences from Dolphin</title>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>There are always two sides. Dolphin's split view, with its toggle and the choice of what to close, is gone.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Each side has its own tabs. In Dolphin the tabs belong to the window.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>The ribbon takes the place of the menu bar, the toolbar and the hamburger menu.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>The Folders panel is gone, as the Miller columns show the parent folders. Places and Terminal are still there.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Typing filters the list instead of jumping to the first match.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Double-clicking on empty space goes up a folder instead of selecting everything.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Nothing is sent to KDE. The telemetry of Dolphin, the download of service menus from the KDE Store, and the help menu entries that lead to KDE's websites are removed.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</chapter>

